
/*
PROBLEM: MINIMUM NUMBER OF DAYS TO MAKE M BOUQUETS
==================================================

APPROACH: BINARY SEARCH ON THE ANSWER

PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING:
We are given an array 'arr', where arr[i] represents the day on
which the i-th flower blooms. We are also given two integers, m
and k. We need to make m bouquets, and each bouquet requires k
adjacent flowers.

A flower can be used only after it has bloomed. We need to find
the minimum number of days required to make all m bouquets.
Each flower can be used in only one bouquet.

CHECKING WHETHER A SOLUTION IS POSSIBLE:
The minimum number of flowers needed is m * k. If m * k is greater
than the total number of flowers, making all the bouquets is
impossible, so we return -1.

We use 1LL * m * k to perform the multiplication as long long,
which helps prevent integer overflow.

HELPER FUNCTION f():
The helper function checks whether we can make m bouquets by a
given day. It goes through the array and checks which flowers
have bloomed by that day.

We use k to count how many more adjacent flowers are needed for
the current bouquet. The variable temp stores the original value
of k so that we can reset the counter after making a bouquet.

If arr[i] <= day, the flower has bloomed, so we decrease k.
When k becomes zero, we have found k consecutive bloomed flowers
and can make one bouquet. We decrease m and reset k to temp for
the next bouquet.

If m becomes zero, all required bouquets have been made, so we
return true immediately.

If a flower has not bloomed by the given day, we reset k to temp.
This is necessary because flowers on opposite sides of an
unbloomed flower cannot belong to the same bouquet.

If we finish checking the array without making all m bouquets,
the function returns false.

SEARCH SPACE:
The earliest possible day is 1, so start = 1. The latest required
blooming day is max(arr), because by that day all flowers have
bloomed. Therefore, the answer lies between 1 and max(arr).

WHY BINARY SEARCH WORKS:
If we can make all bouquets by a particular day, we can also make
them on any later day because flowers that have bloomed remain
available. If we cannot make them by a particular day, we cannot
make them on any earlier day.

This creates a monotonic pattern: earlier days may be invalid,
while later days eventually become valid. We use binary search
to find the first day on which making all bouquets is possible.

BINARY SEARCH LOGIC:
We calculate mid and call f(arr, mid, m, k).

If the helper function returns true, mid is a possible answer.
We store it in ans and search the left half using end = mid - 1
to find an earlier valid day.

If the helper function returns false, we need more time for the
flowers to bloom, so we search the right half using start = mid + 1.

We repeat until start > end and return ans, which stores the
minimum number of days required.

EXAMPLE:
Consider arr = {1, 10, 3, 10, 2}, m = 3, and k = 1.
We need three bouquets, each requiring one flower. By day 3,
the flowers with blooming days 1, 3, and 2 are available, so
we can make all three bouquets. The minimum day is 3.

COMPLEXITY:
Let n be the number of flowers and M be the maximum blooming day.

Time Complexity: O(n * log M), because each helper-function call
takes O(n) time and binary search takes O(log M) iterations.

Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(1), because we use only a fixed
number of extra variables.
*/


// ORIGINAL CODE WITH SHORT COMMENTS

class Solution {
public:
    // Check whether m bouquets can be made by the given day.
    bool f(vector<int>& arr, int day, int m, int k) {
        int n = arr.size();
        int temp = k;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int ele = arr[i];

            if (ele <= day) {
                k--;

                if (!k) {
                    k = temp; // Reset for the next bouquet.
                    m--;

                    if (!m) {
                        return true; // All bouquets are made.
                    }
                }
            } else {
                k = temp; // Break the consecutive sequence.
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

    // Find the minimum day using binary search.
    int minDays(vector<int>& arr, int m, int k) {
        int n = arr.size();
        int start = 1;
        int end = *max_element(arr.begin(), arr.end());
        int ans = -1;

        // Check whether enough flowers exist.
        if (1LL * m * k > n) {
            return -1;
        }

        while (start <= end) {
            int mid = start + (end - start) / 2;
            bool flag = f(arr, mid, m, k);

            if (flag) {
                ans = mid;       // Valid day: search earlier.
                end = mid - 1;
            } else {
                start = mid + 1; // Invalid day: search later.
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
